    "text": "All the evidence is in hand. Here is the analysis, with repo-relative paths from this checkout (`/workspace`).\n\n## Setup: what `zvec.init(optimize_threads=4)` establishes\n\n- `python/zvec/zvec.py:29\u201338, 162\u2013163` \u2014 `init()` puts `optimize_threads` into the config dict and calls `Initialize(config_dict)`.\n- `src/binding/python/model/common/python_config.cc:154\u2013157, 193` \u2014 the binding sets `ConfigData::optimize_thread_count = 4` and calls `GlobalConfig::Instance().initialize(data)`.\n- `src/db/common/global_resource.cc:21\u201329` (accessor at `src/db/common/global_resource.h:31\u201334`) \u2014 a `std::call_once` `initialize()` constructs `optimize_thread_pool_` as `ailego::ThreadPool(optimize_thread_count, ...)`, i.e. the **global optimization pool with 4 workers**. `ailego::ThreadPool` clamps the requested size to `[1, hardware_concurrency]` (`src/ailego/parallel/thread_pool.cc:85\u2013101`), so with hardware_concurrency \u2265 4 the count stays 4.\n\n`collection.optimize(OptimizeOption(concurrency=N))` flows: binding `src/binding/python/model/python_collection.cc:240` \u2192 `OptimizeOptions{concurrency_}` (`src/include/zvec/db/options.h:60\u201362`) \u2192 `src/db/collection.cc:988\u2013990` (`build_compact_task(..., options.concurrency_, ...)`) \u2192 `src/db/index/segment/segment_helper.cc:148\u2013150` (`ReduceVectorIndex(..., task.concurrency_, ...)`) \u2192 `SegmentHelper::MergeWithOptionalReuse`.\n\n## 1. Pool and worker-count selection per invocation\n\nThe decision point is `MergeWithOptionalReuse`, `src/db/index/segment/segment_helper.cc:799\u2013811`:\n\n- **A (`concurrency=0`)**: lines 805\u2013808 \u2014 `merge_options.pool = GlobalResource::Instance().optimize_thread_pool()` and `write_concurrency = pool->count()` = **4**. The merge runs on the **shared global 4-worker optimize pool**.\n- **B (`concurrency=2`)**: lines 809\u2013811 \u2014 `write_concurrency = 2` and `pool` stays `nullptr`. Downstream, `Index::merge` (`src/core/interface/index.cc:1433\u20131446`) sees `options.pool == nullptr` and constructs a **fresh, merge-local `ailego::ThreadPool(2, false)`** held by a `unique_ptr` scoped to the merge call (line 1434, comment included), then `reducer->set_thread_pool(local_thread_pool.get())` (1443). For A, it instead does `reducer->set_thread_pool(options.pool)` and caps `effective_write_concurrency = min(write_concurrency, pool->count())` = min(4,4) = 4 (1436\u20131439).\n\nThe options are forwarded verbatim by `VectorColumnIndexer::merge` (`src/db/index/column/vector_column/vector_column_indexer.cc:128\u2013130`) using `MergeOptions{write_concurrency, pool}` (`src/include/zvec/core/interface/index_param.h:54\u201357`). For a builder-backed target such as DiskANN, `DiskAnnIndex::merge` (`src/core/interface/indexes/diskann_index.cc:361\u2013374`) delegates to `Index::merge`, which registers the builder with the reducer (`src/core/interface/index.cc:1506\u20131508`); `MixedStreamerReducer::reduce` then takes the builder path (`src/core/mixed_reducer/mixed_streamer_reducer.cc:206\u2013233`, `reduce_with_builder` at 672).\n\n**Does B change the global pool? No.** `GlobalResource` is referenced only in the `concurrency == 0` branch (`segment_helper.cc:806`); B's path never touches it, its pool is created once via `call_once` (`global_resource.cc:22\u201323`), and nothing in the merge path mutates it. After B, the global optimize pool still exists with 4 workers; B's local 2-worker pool is destroyed when `Index::merge` returns.\n\n## 2. Handoff to the target builder's train/build and the conversion-train call\n\nAll three calls happen in `MixedStreamerReducer::index_build`, `src/core/mixed_reducer/mixed_streamer_reducer.cc:742\u2013777`:\n\n- Lines 743\u2013744: the selected `ailego::ThreadPool` (global 4-worker pool for A; local 2-worker pool for B) is wrapped in `BorrowedSingleQueueIndexThreads(*thread_pool_)` \u2014 a non-owning `IndexThreads` adapter whose `submit`/`make_group`/`count` forward to the underlying pool (`src/include/zvec/core/framework/index_threads.h:174\u2013225`).\n- Lines 745\u2013747 (optional conversion training, immediately before train/build): `IndexConverter::TrainAndTransform(target_builder_converter_, target_holder, threads)`, which passes the same `threads` into `converter->train(two_pass_holder, std::move(threads))` (`src/core/framework/index_converter.cc:27\u201336`).\n- Line 760: `target_builder_->train(threads, target_holder)`.\n- Line 768: `target_builder_->build(std::move(threads), target_holder)`.\n\nConcretely for DiskANN: `DiskAnnBuilder::train` (`src/core/algorithm/diskann/diskann_builder.cc:633\u2013678`) receives a non-null `threads`, so its own fallback pool (lines 657\u2013664, `SingleQueueIndexThreads(build_thread_count_, false)`) is **not** created, and it calls `train_quantized_data(threads)` (665). `DiskAnnBuilder::build` has the same null-only fallback (714\u2013717), and its graph stages reuse the passed pool directly: `build_internal` submits `threads->count()` tasks via `threads->make_group()` (304\u2013312), as does `prune_internal` (341\u2013351).\n\nDistinction the question asks for: the source proves the **handoff** \u2014 the same pool object selected in step 1 is reachable inside these calls through the borrowed `IndexThreads` wrapper. It does not prove every possible builder/converter implementation *consumes* it; an implementation may ignore the parameter or spawn nested pools. DiskANN's PQ training is exactly such a case:\n\n## 3. DiskANN PQ training: its outer chunk-dispatch pool is **not** reused\n\nIn `DiskAnnBuilder::train_quantized_data` (`src/core/algorithm/diskann/diskann_builder.cc:378\u2013427`):\n\n- Lines 389\u2013394: it creates a `PqInt8Quantizer` and sets `qp.set(\"thread_count\", pq_thread_count)` where\n  `pq_thread_count = max(1, min(build_thread_count_, threads->count()))` \u2014 with the in-source comment \"*The quantizer creates its own pool, so bound it by the caller's pool.*\"\n- `build_thread_count_` is read from `PARAM_DISKANN_BUILDER_THREAD_COUNT` at init and defaults to `max(1, hardware_concurrency)` when unset (`diskann_builder.cc:51\u201355`). Nothing in the collection-optimize path sets that param \u2014 `DiskAnnIndex::create_and_init_streamer` sets only max_degree/list_size/max_pq_chunk_num (`src/core/interface/indexes/diskann_index.cc:101\u2013106`) \u2014 so under the stated assumptions `build_thread_count_ = hardware_concurrency \u2265 4`.\n- The quantizer stores it (`src/turbo/quantizer/pq_int8_quantizer/pq_int8_quantizer.cc:142`; member default 0 at `pq_int8_quantizer.h:217`), and `PqInt8Quantizer::train_all_chunks` builds a **brand-new** `SingleQueueIndexThreads(thread_count_, false)` and distributes PQ chunks round-robin across its `threads->count()` workers (`pq_int8_quantizer.cc:319\u2013340`). `SingleQueueIndexThreads` owns its own `ailego::ThreadPool` (`index_threads.h:112\u2013116, 166`).\n\nSo the PQ-training outer pool is a distinct, quantizer-owned pool, not the borrowed caller pool. Its worker count is `max(1, min(builder's build_thread_count_, caller pool count))`: **4 for A** (min(hw, 4), hw \u2265 4) and **2 for B** (min(hw, 2)). The caller's pool influences it only through the `threads->count()` bound while the caller's pool is idle-waiting; the builder's own setting supplies the ceiling. (By contrast, DiskANN's graph build/prune stages in `build` do reuse the borrowed pool directly \u2014 lines 304\u2013312, 341\u2013351.)\n\n## 4. What these settings establish \u2014 and what they don't\n\n**Established (worker-pool capacity):**\n- A merges run on the global optimize pool with exactly **4 workers** (`optimize_threads=4` \u2192 `global_resource.cc:27\u201329`; borrowed at `index.cc:1436\u20131439`), and the reducer's add-thread parameter is capped to 4 (`index.cc:1448\u20131452`).\n- B merges run on a per-call local pool with exactly **2 workers**, destroyed when the merge returns (`index.cc:1434, 1441\u20131445`); the global pool remains configured at 4 and is untouched.\n- For a DiskANN PQ-training stage, an additional dedicated pool exists with capacity min(hardware_concurrency, caller-pool count) \u2192 4 under A, 2 under B (`diskann_builder.cc:391\u2013394`, `pq_int8_quantizer.cc:322\u2013328`).\n\n**Not established:**\n- **Whole-process thread count.** These settings bound only the pools they configure. The process also holds the global query pool (`global_resource.cc:24\u201326`), any pools other builders/quantizers/converters create internally (as PQ training does), per-merge local pools from concurrent operations, plus Python-runtime and third-party threads. Nothing here sums to a process-wide thread total, and pool sizes are upper bounds on capacity, not counts of threads doing work.\n- **Actual CPU utilization.** Whether the 4 or 2 workers are busy depends on runtime behavior the configuration can't determine: task granularity, queueing in `SingleQueueTaskGroup` (`index_threads.h:90\u201395`), DiskANN's disk I/O waits, and the fact that nested pools (e.g., PQ training's own pool) run while the outer pool's workers are blocked waiting \u2014 so a \"4-worker\" merge does not imply 4 cores saturated, and the `min()` bounds cap capacity without guaranteeing it is ever reached."
  },
